{
  "$comment": "The area registry (docs/test-explorer-spec.md, \"The area registry\"). Rows are feature areas in product language, matching the outer `describe` convention in testing-strategy.md's \"Tests are named in the product's language, not the implementation's\" - undotted, not an entity ('Triage', not 'item.setStatus'). Labels are in the product's language, and docs/functional-definition.md's Glossary is where the words come from: a behaviour the Glossary has no word for gains one in the same change, as Menus, Deleting, Ordering, Selection, Live updates and Updating did. Not every label is itself a Glossary entry - some name an activity around one ('Workspace management'), and 'Across the app' and 'Infrastructure' name where a row sits rather than something the product does. A source file can match more than one area's sourcePatterns (apps/api/src/domain/items.ts backs Capture, Triage and Offline) - expected, not an overlap error; a file matching none falls into 'infrastructure' automatically. `pnpm --filter @cockpit/test-explorer check-concepts` fails when a test declares an area missing from this file, and tests/integration/concepts-registry.test.js fails when a non-glob pattern here names a file the analyzer does not walk - so renaming a source file is a change to this file too. An empty sourcePatterns list declares an area ahead of the code that will own it, and holds the row at zero until then - the only thing that puts a row on the page with no test behind it. `parent` nests one entry under another, which is what makes the rows read as the product's own containment (Workspace > Inbox/Dashboards > Panels, per the functional definition's \"Container hierarchy\"). Workspaces, Inbox and Across the app hold other rows and own no code themselves; a row that holds others shows its own count and its subtree's, so collapsing one never reads as untested. `browserWalks` is that area's ceiling on F3 walks, held by `scripts/e2e-ceilings.mjs` rather than by anything this tool does (\"Bring the browser tier back inside a budget\", issue 509); docs/test-explorer-spec.md's \"The area registry\" is where it is written down.",
  "e2eShardBudget": {
    "seconds": 720,
    "typicalSeconds": 420,
    "reason": "The most Playwright's reported duration (stack boot included, install excluded) may be for one E2E (F3) shard's walks, held by scripts/e2e-budget.mjs. Set from two full sharded runs an hour apart: the slowest shard (1) took 6.0 and 8.0 minutes, typical 7 minutes (420 s). 720 s = 12 minutes = 1.7 times that typical and 1.5 times the worst run seen, so a run 30% slower under runner load still passes and a doubling (14 minutes) fails. Catches a doubling, not drift. Re-read from a week of sharded runs.",
    "$comment": "`seconds` is the budget and `typicalSeconds` the slowest shard's typical it was set above, which scripts/lib/e2e-budget.test.mjs holds to at least 1.3 times (the swing runner load gives a run) and under 2 times (a doubling must fail). Beside `browserWalks`, the per-area ceilings on how many walks there are, because both bound the tier's growth. Raising `seconds` is an edit in the pull request that needs it, with `reason` saying what the tier measured."
  },
